insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('ci-f-1000','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is a brute-force attack, and what determines how long one takes against a symmetric key?$$,$$A brute-force attack tries every possible key value, one at a time, until decryption produces a meaningful plaintext. The time required is governed by the size of the key space (2^n for an n-bit key) and the attacker's available compute/parallelization; each additional key bit doubles the effort, which is why AES-256 is exponentially more brute-force resistant than AES-128, not just twice as strong.$$),
('ci-f-1001','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$How does frequency analysis break a classical substitution cipher such as a Caesar or monoalphabetic cipher?$$,$$Natural-language letter frequencies (in English, letters like E, T, and A occur far more often than others) survive a simple substitution unchanged in pattern, only relabeled. An analyst maps the most frequent ciphertext symbols to the most frequent plaintext letters and reconstructs the substitution key without ever guessing it directly. Modern block and stream ciphers defeat frequency analysis by diffusing and confusing plaintext statistics so no such pattern survives into the ciphertext.$$),
('ci-f-1002','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is a known-plaintext attack (KPA)?$$,$$The attacker possesses one or more matched plaintext/ciphertext pairs that they did not choose, but that occurred through normal operation (such as a predictable message header or standard greeting), and uses those pairs to deduce the key or the algorithm's internal state. A classic example is WWII codebreakers exploiting the fact that many intercepted Enigma messages began with predictable text, such as a routine daily weather report.$$),
('ci-f-1003','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is a chosen-plaintext attack (CPA), and how is it more powerful than a known-plaintext attack?$$,$$In a CPA, the attacker can submit arbitrary plaintexts of their own choosing to an encryption oracle and observe the resulting ciphertext, rather than only having whatever pairs happen to occur naturally as in a known-plaintext attack. That active control lets the attacker probe the cipher with carefully crafted inputs designed to expose exploitable patterns; a modern cipher is expected to resist CPA (a property called IND-CPA security) as a baseline requirement.$$),
('ci-f-1004','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$In a cryptographic context, what does a man-in-the-middle (MITM) attack against a key exchange achieve, and what defense stops it?$$,$$The attacker secretly relays, and can alter, communications between two parties who believe they are negotiating directly with each other - for example, intercepting an unauthenticated Diffie-Hellman exchange and substituting their own key material with each side, so they can decrypt, read, and re-encrypt all resulting traffic. The defense is authenticating the exchange itself, such as signing the key-exchange parameters with a certificate or using mutual TLS, so each party can verify who they are really negotiating with.$$),
('ci-f-1005','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is a side-channel attack, and how does it differ from mathematical cryptanalysis?$$,$$Rather than attacking the mathematics of an algorithm, a side-channel attack exploits information leaked by its physical implementation - timing variations, power draw, electromagnetic emissions, sound, or cache-access patterns - to infer secret key material. A cipher can be mathematically unbreakable and still be defeated this way, which is why constant-time implementations and hardware shielding are essential engineering practices, not optional extras.$$),
('ci-f-1006','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$How does a timing attack recover secret key bits from a cryptographic implementation?$$,$$Many naive implementations take measurably different amounts of time depending on the secret key value being processed, for example a modular-exponentiation routine that branches based on individual key bits. By statistically measuring response times across many repeated operations, an attacker can infer the key bit by bit. The countermeasure is a constant-time algorithm that always performs the same sequence of operations regardless of the secret data's value.$$),
('ci-f-1007','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is the difference between simple power analysis (SPA) and differential power analysis (DPA)?$$,$$SPA directly reads a device's power-consumption trace and visually or directly correlates observable patterns in it to the operations being executed, such as spotting a distinct pattern for a key bit of 1 versus 0 during RSA exponentiation. DPA is more powerful and statistical: it collects many traces and applies correlation analysis across all of them, extracting keys even when the per-operation power differences are far too subtle to see by inspecting any single trace.$$),
('ci-f-1008','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is a birthday attack, and why does it threaten hash functions specifically?$$,$$It exploits the birthday paradox - the counterintuitive fact that in a group of just 23 people there is over a 50% chance two share a birthday, because every pair is being compared, not one fixed target against everyone else. Applied to hashing, an attacker only needs to search roughly 2^(n/2) inputs, not 2^n, to find any two that collide, since any match among many candidates counts. This effectively halves a hash function's security margin, which is why digest lengths are chosen to be roughly double a comparable symmetric key's length.$$),
('ci-f-1009','cissp','core',3,'3.7',$$What is a rainbow table attack, and what single control most effectively defeats it?$$,$$A rainbow table is a precomputed set of chained hash-to-plaintext mappings that lets an attacker look up a captured password hash almost instantly instead of hashing every candidate password in real time. Salting - appending a unique random value to each password before hashing, so identical passwords no longer produce identical hashes - defeats it, because the attacker would need a separate precomputed table for every possible salt value, making precomputation infeasible at scale.$$),
('ci-a-340','cissp','acronym',3,'3.7',$$MITM$$,$$Man-In-The-Middle - an attack in which the adversary secretly intercepts and can alter communications between two parties who believe they are communicating directly, such as substituting key-exchange material in an unauthenticated Diffie-Hellman handshake.$$),
('ci-a-341','cissp','acronym',3,'3.7',$$CPA$$,$$Chosen-Plaintext Attack - the attacker can submit arbitrary plaintexts of their choosing to an encryption oracle and observe the resulting ciphertext, giving them more control than a known-plaintext attack; resisting CPA is a baseline requirement for modern ciphers.$$),
('ci-a-342','cissp','acronym',3,'3.7',$$KPA$$,$$Known-Plaintext Attack - the attacker has one or more matched plaintext/ciphertext pairs that occurred naturally (not chosen by the attacker) and uses them to deduce the key or the cipher's internal state.$$),
('ci-a-343','cissp','acronym',3,'3.7',$$COA$$,$$Ciphertext-Only Attack - the attacker has only captured ciphertext, with no corresponding plaintext at all, making it the weakest attack model an attacker can operate under and the hardest one to succeed with.$$),
('ci-a-344','cissp','acronym',3,'3.7',$$DPA$$,$$Differential Power Analysis - a side-channel technique that applies statistical correlation across many collected power-consumption traces of a device performing the same operation, extracting a secret key even when no single trace reveals it by inspection.$$),
('ci-y-210','cissp','feynman',3,'3.7',$$Explain why the birthday attack means a hash function's output needs to be roughly twice as long as an equivalent symmetric key, as if teaching a beginner.$$,$$Model answer: Picture a room full of people and ask "what are the odds two share a birthday?" You might think you'd need close to 365 people, but you only need about 23, because you're not checking one specific person against everyone else - you're checking every possible pair against every other pair, and there are a lot more pairs than people. A birthday attack on a hash works the same way: an attacker isn't trying to find an input that matches one specific target hash (that really would take about 2^n tries for an n-bit hash). They just want any two inputs, out of however many they try, that happen to collide with each other - and because they're comparing all pairs, that happens after roughly the square root of the total possibilities, or about 2^(n/2) tries. So if a hash's collision resistance is only as strong as 2^(n/2), designers double the output length compared to what they'd need for a straightforward guess-a-target attack, to keep that effectively-halved security margin at a safe level.$$),
('ci-y-211','cissp','feynman',3,'3.7',$$Explain the practical difference between a side-channel attack and breaking the cipher's underlying math, using a padlock analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a padlock that is mathematically perfect - no one has ever picked its exact mechanism through the keyhole, no matter how skilled. Breaking the cipher's math would be like finding a way to pick that lock through the keyhole itself. A side-channel attack skips the keyhole entirely: instead, you notice that when you turn the correct combination, the lock makes a subtly different click sound, or takes a hair longer to turn, than when you try a wrong one. You never touch the "official" attack surface at all - you're listening to the machine doing the work and reading its unintentional physical tells. That's exactly what timing and power analysis do to a cipher: the math stays unbroken, but the chip running it leaks clues about the secret through how long it takes or how much electricity it draws while it works, and those clues are enough to reconstruct the key without ever solving the underlying cryptographic problem.$$),
('ci-y-212','cissp','feynman',3,'3.7',$$Explain why an attacker limited to a known-plaintext attack is weaker than one capable of a chosen-plaintext attack, walking through what each attacker can and cannot control.$$,$$Model answer: Think of it like being handed old exam questions versus being allowed to write your own practice questions and see the answer key for each one. A known-plaintext attacker is handed whatever plaintext/ciphertext pairs happen to already exist - maybe a predictable letterhead, maybe a routine status message - but they have zero say over what those pairs actually are. They have to work with whatever scraps show up. A chosen-plaintext attacker gets to design their own experiment: they can submit a carefully crafted plaintext specifically engineered to expose a weakness - say, a string designed to trigger an edge case in the cipher's internal rounds - and immediately see the resulting ciphertext. That freedom to actively probe the system, instead of passively waiting for useful pairs to appear, is what makes CPA a strictly stronger attack model, and it's exactly why cryptographers require a modern cipher to resist chosen-plaintext attacks, not merely known-plaintext ones.$$),
('ci-f-1010','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$What are the three original CPTED strategies, and how does each discourage crime through design rather than force?$$,$$Natural surveillance designs sightlines and lighting so legitimate occupants can casually observe an area during normal activity. Natural access control uses walkways, landscaping, and entrance placement to guide people through a small number of well-observed paths, rather than relying on fences or guards to stop them. Territorial reinforcement uses signage, landscaping, and clear ownership cues so a space visibly "belongs" to someone, signaling to an offender that their presence will be noticed and is not welcome. All three work by shaping behavior and perception through the physical environment itself, rather than through mechanical barriers or force.$$),
('ci-f-1011','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$What is Oscar Newman's defensible space theory, and how does it relate to CPTED?$$,$$Newman's 1970s theory holds that residents will naturally protect a space if its physical design gives them a sense of ownership and the ability to observe it, and identified four contributing elements: territoriality, natural surveillance, image/milieu (a well-maintained appearance that signals a cared-for space), and safe adjoining areas (proximity to other well-used, low-crime zones). Defensible space theory is considered the direct intellectual predecessor of CPTED, which turned these ideas into specific, applicable design strategies.$$),
('ci-f-1012','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$What is the difference between CPTED's natural access control and traditional mechanical or organized access control?$$,$$Natural access control shapes movement through design elements alone - the placement of entrances, walkways, landscaping, and grade changes that guide people through a small number of well-observed paths, without needing a person or device to enforce it. Mechanical access control (locks, card readers, turnstiles) and organized access control (security guards) actively enforce entry decisions instead of relying on design and perception. CPTED prioritizes natural methods first, then layers mechanical or organized controls on top for the specific points that still need hard enforcement.$$),
('ci-f-1013','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$Why is target hardening (bars, heavy locks, imposing fencing) generally treated as distinct from, and sometimes in tension with, CPTED's core natural strategies?$$,$$Target hardening relies on visible physical barriers and force to deny access, which can create a fortress-like appearance that increases perceived fear and can undermine the casual surveillance and territorial cues CPTED depends on. CPTED's natural strategies aim to prevent crime through design and social cues before force is ever needed; target hardening is sometimes still necessary in high-risk areas, but it is treated as a fallback layer added on top of - not a substitute for - natural surveillance, access control, and territorial reinforcement.$$),
('ci-f-1014','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$How does broken windows theory relate to CPTED's territorial reinforcement strategy?$$,$$Broken windows theory holds that visible signs of disorder and neglect - broken windows, graffiti, litter - signal that no one is watching or cares, inviting further disorder and more serious crime. Territorial reinforcement counters this directly: well-maintained landscaping, prompt repair, clean common areas, and clear ownership markers signal an actively cared-for space, discouraging opportunistic offenders before any incident occurs.$$),
('ci-f-1015','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$What are the four generic zones typically used to apply layered, concentric physical security design around a facility, from outermost to innermost?$$,$$Public/uncontrolled zone (surrounding grounds, parking, sidewalks - open to anyone), controlled zone (building perimeter and lobby, where access begins to be checked), restricted zone (interior areas limited to authorized staff, such as office floors), and security/critical zone (the highest-sensitivity areas like a data center or server room, requiring the strongest authentication). Each zone should require progressively stronger authentication and monitoring - the same defense-in-depth logic applied to physical space rather than to a network.$$),
('ci-f-1016','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$What three lighting types are commonly specified in a CPTED-informed exterior lighting design, and what does each do?$$,$$Continuous lighting stays on at a fixed level throughout hours of darkness, providing constant illumination and deterrence. Standby lighting activates on a schedule or in response to suspected activity to supplement continuous lighting where needed. Movable or trigger-activated (motion-sensor) lighting turns on only when motion is detected, saving energy while also drawing attention to activity in an otherwise dark area.$$),
('ci-f-1017','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$How does landscaping design support natural surveillance, and what is the general height guidance for shrubs and tree canopies near a facility?$$,$$Landscaping should preserve sightlines rather than create hiding spots or blind corners: low shrubs and ground cover are generally kept under about 2-3 feet so they don't obscure a view across them, while tree canopies are trimmed above roughly 6-8 feet so a low, sight-blocking layer doesn't form at eye level near windows, walkways, and parking areas.$$),
('ci-f-1018','cissp','core',3,'3.8',$$What is crime displacement, and what does it suggest about how CPTED programs should be applied?$$,$$Crime displacement is the documented critique that hardening or redesigning one location to reduce crime there may simply relocate the same offenders and behavior to a nearby, less-protected location, rather than eliminating the underlying criminal activity. It suggests CPTED is most effective when applied consistently across an area or neighborhood rather than to a single isolated site, since an isolated hardening effort can just push the problem next door.$$),
('ci-a-345','cissp','acronym',3,'3.8',$$CPTED$$,$$Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design - a site and facility design philosophy that discourages crime through the physical environment itself, using natural surveillance, natural access control, and territorial reinforcement rather than force alone.$$),
('ci-a-346','cissp','acronym',3,'3.8',$$PPS$$,$$Physical Protection System - the overall framework for a site's physical security, typically evaluated on its ability to detect, delay, and respond to an adversary before the adversary completes a malicious act.$$),
('ci-a-347','cissp','acronym',3,'3.8',$$SBD$$,$$Secured By Design - a police-endorsed crime-prevention accreditation scheme, closely related to CPTED, that certifies buildings and developments meeting defined physical security and layout standards intended to design out opportunities for crime.$$),
('ci-a-348','cissp','acronym',3,'3.8',$$ASIS$$,$$ASIS International - the leading professional association for physical security practitioners, which publishes widely referenced physical security, CPTED-adjacent, and facility protection guidelines and standards.$$),
('ci-y-213','cissp','feynman',3,'3.8',$$Explain the three original CPTED strategies using a simple retail store example.$$,$$Model answer: Picture a small retail store redesigning its front lot. Natural surveillance means trimming the hedges below window height and adding clear glass storefronts, so an employee at the register can casually see the whole parking lot just by glancing up - no camera required, just a clear line of sight during normal work. Natural access control means routing customers along one obvious, well-lit path from the parking lot to the front door, instead of letting people wander in from three different unlit side entrances where no one would notice them. Territorial reinforcement means putting up a clean sign, keeping the landscaping trimmed, and repainting the curb, so the lot visibly looks cared-for and "owned" rather than abandoned - which quietly tells a would-be offender that someone is paying attention to this place. None of the three involves a lock, a guard, or a fence; they all just make anyone up to something feel more exposed.$$),
('ci-y-214','cissp','feynman',3,'3.8',$$Explain why target hardening (bars, heavy fences) is not the same thing as CPTED, using a plain-language contrast.$$,$$Model answer: Target hardening is the "brute force" answer to physical security: put steel bars on every window and razor wire on every fence, and hope raw physical resistance keeps people out. CPTED asks a different question first: can we design the space so fewer people ever want to try in the first place, and so anyone who does try feels watched and out of place? A building wrapped in bars and razor wire actually works against some of CPTED's own goals - it announces fear, it can block the very sightlines that support natural surveillance, and a fortress look can make legitimate visitors feel unwelcome too. That doesn't mean target hardening is wrong; a bank vault genuinely needs a hardened door. It just means CPTED treats hardening as a targeted, last-resort layer for genuinely high-risk points, applied after the natural design strategies have already done as much of the work as they can.$$),
('ci-y-215','cissp','feynman',3,'3.8',$$Explain Oscar Newman's defensible space theory as if teaching a new security analyst.$$,$$Model answer: Newman studied public housing in the 1970s and noticed something: buildings with nearly identical crime rates on paper had very different real-world outcomes depending on how residents felt about the space around them. Where residents felt the courtyard was "theirs" - because it was visible from their windows, well-kept, and clearly belonged to their building rather than to the whole block - they watched over it, and it stayed safer. Where the space felt anonymous, unclaimed, and impossible to see from any apartment, nobody felt responsible for it, and it became a magnet for crime nobody stopped. Newman boiled that observation down into four levers a designer can pull: territoriality (does this space visibly belong to someone), natural surveillance (can it be casually observed), image/milieu (does it look cared-for), and safe adjoining areas (is it near other spaces that are already well-used and safe). CPTED is essentially Newman's theory turned into a practical design checklist.$$),
('ci-y-216','cissp','feynman',3,'3.8',$$Explain how landscaping and lighting choices work together to support natural surveillance, avoiding jargon.$$,$$Model answer: Natural surveillance only works if people can actually see what's happening, in both directions and at both times of day. During the day, that's a landscaping problem: a hedge that's grown too tall, or a tree canopy that's dropped too low, can quietly turn a wide-open courtyard into a row of hidden pockets nobody can see into from a window or a passing car. At night, the same problem becomes a lighting problem, since even perfectly trimmed landscaping is useless if the area is pitch black. That's why the two get planned together rather than separately: keep shrubs low and tree canopies high so sightlines survive the growing season, and layer in lighting - a steady baseline plus motion-triggered lights in the darker corners - so those same sightlines still work after sunset. Get either one wrong on its own, and the other one's work gets undone.$$),
('ci-y-217','cissp','feynman',3,'3.8',$$Explain layered, concentric physical security zones using an onion analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Think of a facility like an onion, with the most sensitive asset - say, the server room - sitting at the very center. The outermost layer is the parking lot and sidewalks: public, essentially uncontrolled, anyone can walk there. Peel that back and you hit the building perimeter and lobby - the controlled zone, where a badge or a receptionist starts checking who you are. Peel further and you're on a restricted office floor, open only to employees who work there. Keep peeling and you reach the core: the security/critical zone, like the server room, that demands the strongest possible check - maybe a biometric scan - before the door opens at all. Just like an onion, nobody expects the outer skin to be as tough as the core; each layer only has to be strong enough to slow someone down and force them through the next check, and the layers stack their strength together rather than each one having to stop an attacker alone.$$),
('ci-f-1019','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What is the difference between ionization and photoelectric smoke detectors, and which responds faster to which kind of fire?$$,$$Ionization detectors use a small radioactive source to ionize air between two charged plates; smoke particles disrupt the current and trigger the alarm, making them faster to respond to fast, flaming fires that produce small particles. Photoelectric detectors shine a light beam and detect scattering caused by smoke, making them faster to respond to smoldering, slow-burning fires that produce larger, denser smoke particles before open flame appears. Many modern detectors combine both technologies for balanced coverage.$$),
('ci-f-1020','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What is the difference between wet pipe, dry pipe, pre-action, and deluge sprinkler systems?$$,$$Wet pipe systems keep pipes constantly filled with pressurized water, discharging immediately when a sprinkler head's heat-sensitive element triggers - fastest response, but highest accidental-discharge risk. Dry pipe systems hold pressurized air or nitrogen instead of water until a head triggers, delaying water entry, and are used where freezing is a risk. Pre-action systems require two separate triggers - a detection-system alarm AND an individual sprinkler head trigger - before water even enters the pipes, minimizing accidental-discharge damage in sensitive spaces like data centers. Deluge systems use open, non-heat-triggered sprinkler heads that release water across an entire zone at once when a separate detection system activates, used for high-hazard areas needing rapid, total coverage.$$),
('ci-f-1021','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$How do Halon, CO2, and modern clean agents (FM-200, Novec 1230, inert-gas blends) compare as fire-suppression agents for occupied data centers?$$,$$Halon extinguishes fire effectively through chemical chain-reaction interruption without damaging electronics, but was phased out under the Montreal Protocol because it depletes stratospheric ozone. CO2 suppresses fire by displacing oxygen below the level that supports combustion, which is effective and non-damaging to equipment, but that same oxygen displacement can asphyxiate occupants, requiring an evacuation delay before discharge. Modern clean agents such as FM-200 and Novec 1230, and inert-gas blends like Inergen, suppress fire similarly to Halon without its ozone impact, and are engineered to remain safe at their effective concentration for brief human exposure - making them the preferred choice for occupied spaces.$$),
('ci-f-1022','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$Why do data centers maintain positive air pressure and a controlled relative humidity band, roughly 40-60%?$$,$$Positive pressurization keeps unfiltered outside air, dust, and contaminants from being drawn in through door openings and gaps, protecting sensitive equipment from particulate buildup. Humidity control balances two opposing risks: air that is too dry lets static electricity build up, risking electrostatic-discharge damage to components, while air that is too humid promotes condensation and corrosion; the 40-60% relative humidity band is the generally recommended middle ground.$$),
('ci-f-1023','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What role does each of these play in a layered facility power-protection strategy: UPS, backup generator, PDU, and EPO switch?$$,$$A UPS uses batteries to provide near-instantaneous power at the moment of an outage, bridging the gap with zero interruption. A backup generator takes longer to start, seconds to a few minutes, but can sustain power for hours or days on fuel once running. A Power Distribution Unit (PDU) distributes conditioned power from the main feed out to individual racks and equipment. An Emergency Power Off (EPO) switch lets responders immediately cut all power to a room during a fire or electrical emergency for life-safety reasons - a deliberate availability trade-off made in favor of protecting human life.$$),
('ci-f-1024','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What is 2N power redundancy, and how does it differ from N+1 redundancy?$$,$$N+1 redundancy provides one spare unit beyond the number needed to meet normal load, for example three UPS units when only two are needed, tolerating a single component failure. 2N redundancy fully duplicates the entire power path - two complete, independent power systems (utility feeds, UPS, generators, distribution), each independently sized to carry the full load - so an entire path can fail, or be taken offline for maintenance, while the other continues carrying 100% of the load without any capacity shortfall.$$),
('ci-f-1025','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What are the main categories of physical access control locks used at a facility, from least to most sophisticated?$$,$$Mechanical key-and-tumbler locks offer basic control but keys can be copied or lost without an audit trail. Cipher/keypad locks require a shared numeric code, adding no individual accountability since the code is common to everyone who knows it. Electronic smart-card and proximity-badge locks grant access per individual credential and log every entry attempt, providing accountability and centralized revocation. Biometric locks (fingerprint, iris, hand geometry) tie access to a physical characteristic of the person, offering the strongest identity assurance, at higher cost and with enrollment/false-reject considerations.$$),
('ci-f-1026','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What physical facility controls work together to secure a building's perimeter and exterior?$$,$$Fencing (rated by height and construction - taller fencing with outrigger topping deters climbing far more than a low fence), bollards (fixed posts that stop vehicle-borne attacks while leaving pedestrian access and sightlines open), CCTV camera coverage (for deterrence and after-the-fact investigation), and security guard patrols together form layered exterior protection. Each compensates for what the others can't do alone: a fence stops casual intrusion but not vehicles, cameras don't physically stop anyone, and guards provide judgment cameras can't.$$),
('ci-f-1027','cissp','core',3,'3.9',$$What does environmental monitoring in a data center track, and why does it need distributed rather than single-point sensors?$$,$$Environmental monitoring continuously tracks temperature, humidity, water/leak presence (such as near raised-floor plenum and cooling lines), and airborne particulates against defined baselines, alerting staff when conditions drift out of range - often catching a failing HVAC unit or a developing leak before it damages equipment. Distributed sensors placed throughout the room, not just one central reading, are needed because hot spots, localized leaks, and airflow problems can occur in one part of a data hall while the rest of the room reads normal.$$),
('ci-a-349','cissp','acronym',3,'3.9',$$HVAC$$,$$Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning - the systems that control a data center's temperature, humidity, and air pressure, protecting equipment from heat, static-discharge risk from over-dry air, and condensation/corrosion risk from over-humid air.$$),
('ci-a-350','cissp','acronym',3,'3.9',$$UPS$$,$$Uninterruptible Power Supply - a battery-backed device that provides near-instantaneous power at the moment of a utility outage, bridging the gap with zero interruption until a backup generator can start and reach full output.$$),
('ci-a-351','cissp','acronym',3,'3.9',$$EPO$$,$$Emergency Power Off - a manually operated switch, typically near exits, that immediately cuts all power to a room during a fire or electrical emergency to protect responders, at the cost of an abrupt, uncontrolled shutdown of everything in the space.$$),
('ci-a-352','cissp','acronym',3,'3.9',$$PDU$$,$$Power Distribution Unit - equipment that distributes conditioned power from a facility's main feed out to individual racks and devices, often providing per-outlet monitoring and remote power cycling.$$),
('ci-a-353','cissp','acronym',3,'3.9',$$CRAC$$,$$Computer Room Air Conditioner - a dedicated HVAC unit designed specifically to maintain the tight temperature and humidity tolerances a data center requires, distinct from general building climate-control equipment.$$),
('ci-y-218','cissp','feynman',3,'3.9',$$Explain why data centers use pre-action sprinkler systems instead of ordinary wet-pipe sprinklers, as if teaching a facilities manager.$$,$$Model answer: A wet-pipe system is always loaded with water right up to the sprinkler head, so if one head ever fails or gets bumped hard enough, water starts flowing immediately - no way to stop it. In a room full of racks worth millions of dollars, that single point of failure is terrifying. A pre-action system adds a safety interlock: the pipes themselves stay dry, air-filled, until a separate fire-detection system - smoke or heat sensors - confirms something is actually wrong and opens a valve to let water into the pipes. Only then does the individual sprinkler head's own heat trigger matter, exactly like a normal sprinkler would work from there. You've effectively added a second, independent "are you sure?" check before water can ever reach the equipment, which is exactly the layered-verification thinking you want anywhere an accidental discharge is nearly as costly as the fire itself.$$),
('ci-y-219','cissp','feynman',3,'3.9',$$Explain why both a UPS and a generator are needed for power redundancy, using a relay-race analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Think of keeping the lights on during an outage as a relay race with two very different runners. The UPS is the sprinter: the instant utility power drops, it's already running, because its battery has been sitting there fully charged the whole time - there's no lag, no startup delay, just an instant handoff with zero gap. But a sprinter can't run forever; batteries only hold so much charge. The generator is the marathon runner: it takes a bit to get moving, seconds to a couple of minutes to start up and reach stable output, which is far too slow to cover that first instant of an outage on its own. So the sprinter (UPS) carries the load for those first critical seconds while the marathon runner (generator) gets up to speed, and then the baton passes - the generator takes over and can keep going for hours or days on fuel, well past what any battery bank could sustain.$$),
('ci-y-220','cissp','feynman',3,'3.9',$$Explain why humidity control matters for a data center, covering both the too-dry and too-humid failure modes.$$,$$Model answer: Humidity is one of those things that's dangerous at both extremes, not just one. Go too dry, and you've built the perfect conditions for static electricity: technicians walking across a floor or handling components build up a static charge, and dry air lets that charge accumulate instead of slowly bleeding away, so an accidental electrostatic discharge into a sensitive component can silently damage it. Go too humid, and moisture starts condensing on cool surfaces - metal contacts, connectors, circuit boards - which invites corrosion and, in the worst case, direct electrical shorts. The 40-60% relative humidity band that data centers target sits in the middle precisely because it's far enough from either danger zone: dry enough to avoid condensation, humid enough to keep static buildup from becoming a real threat.$$),
('ci-y-221','cissp','feynman',3,'3.9',$$Explain the difference between N+1 and 2N power redundancy using a simple staffing analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a shift that needs exactly two people to run smoothly. N+1 staffing means scheduling three people instead of two - if any one of them calls in sick, you're still fully covered, but you've only budgeted for a single absence at a time; two people out at once and you're short-staffed. 2N staffing is a completely different commitment: you hire and fully train two entirely separate teams of two, each capable of running the whole shift completely on its own, with zero overlap between them. If an entire team walks out - or you deliberately send one team home for training - the other team alone still runs the full shift without missing a beat. That's the real difference: N+1 tolerates losing one piece of a shared pool, while 2N means you could lose an entire independent half of your capacity and still be running at 100%.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('ci-q-1000','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',1,$q$Which cryptanalysis technique exploits the uneven distribution of letters in natural-language plaintext to break a classical substitution cipher?$q$,$q$["Frequency analysis","Brute-force attack","Chosen-ciphertext attack","Birthday attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Frequency analysis relies on the fact that a simple substitution cipher relabels letters but does not change how often each one occurs, so the most common ciphertext symbols can be mapped back to the most common plaintext letters (E, T, A in English) to recover the key. Brute force instead tries every key; chosen-ciphertext requires access to a decryption oracle; a birthday attack targets hash collisions - none of those exploit letter-frequency statistics.$q$),
('ci-q-1001','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',1,$q$An attacker tries every possible key value, one at a time, until decryption of an intercepted message produces meaningful plaintext. Which attack is this?$q$,$q$["A brute-force attack","A known-plaintext attack","A rainbow table attack","A man-in-the-middle attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Exhaustively trying every key in the key space until one produces valid plaintext is the definition of a brute-force attack; its cost is bounded by the size of the key space, which is why key length is chosen to make this exhaustive search infeasible.$q$),
('ci-q-1002','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',2,$q$During WWII, codebreakers exploited the fact that many intercepted Enigma messages began with predictable text, such as a standard daily weather report format, to help recover the day's key settings. Which category of cryptanalysis attack does this best describe?$q$,$q$["A known-plaintext attack","A chosen-plaintext attack","A ciphertext-only attack","A rainbow table attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The codebreakers did not get to choose what plaintext was sent - the predictable weather-report format simply occurred naturally as part of routine operations - but they knew what plaintext corresponded to certain intercepted ciphertext, which is exactly the definition of a known-plaintext attack. A chosen-plaintext attack would require the attacker to control what plaintext was encrypted.$q$),
('ci-q-1003','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',3,$q$A researcher testing a new block cipher is allowed to submit any plaintext of their own choosing to an encryption oracle and observe the resulting ciphertext, repeating this with carefully crafted inputs designed to expose exploitable structure in the cipher's output. Which attack model is the researcher using?$q$,$q$["A chosen-plaintext attack","A known-plaintext attack","A side-channel attack","A birthday attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The defining feature here is that the researcher actively chooses and submits plaintext to an oracle, rather than passively working with plaintext/ciphertext pairs that occur naturally (known-plaintext) or exploiting the physical implementation (side-channel). Differential cryptanalysis of block ciphers is a classic chosen-plaintext technique.$q$),
('ci-q-1004','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',2,$q$Two parties perform an unauthenticated Diffie-Hellman key exchange over a network an attacker fully controls. The attacker intercepts each side's public value and substitutes their own, establishing a separate shared secret with each party without either one noticing. What best describes this attack, and what design gap made it possible?$q$,$q$["A man-in-the-middle attack, made possible by the exchange lacking authentication of the parties' identities","A birthday attack, made possible by a short hash output","A rainbow table attack, made possible by unsalted key storage","A timing attack, made possible by non-constant-time key derivation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Diffie-Hellman alone provides confidentiality of the derived secret against a passive eavesdropper, but it does not by itself authenticate who is on the other end - so an active attacker positioned on the network path can substitute their own key material with each party undetected. Fixing this requires binding the exchange to an authenticated identity, such as signing the exchange parameters with a certificate.$q$),
('ci-q-1005','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following best describes a side-channel attack against a cryptographic system?$q$,$q$["An attack that exploits information leaked by the system's physical implementation, such as timing or power consumption, rather than a mathematical weakness in the algorithm","An attack that exploits weak, human-chosen passwords by trying dictionary words","An attack that exploits an unpatched software vulnerability to gain remote code execution","An attack that forges a digital certificate's signature"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Side-channel attacks target the physical realities of how a cryptographic operation is actually executed - timing, power draw, electromagnetic emissions - rather than any weakness in the algorithm's mathematics, which means even a provably strong cipher can be defeated if its implementation leaks these physical signals.$q$),
('ci-q-1006','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',2,$q$A smart card's RSA implementation performs modular exponentiation using an algorithm whose execution time varies slightly depending on the value of each secret key bit being processed. An attacker who can repeatedly query the card and precisely measure response latency is exploiting which weakness?$q$,$q$["A timing side channel","A birthday collision","A frequency-analysis weakness in the cipher","A weak random number generator"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Statistically correlating measured response latency with the secret key bits being processed is the definition of a timing attack, a specific class of side-channel attack. The countermeasure is a constant-time implementation that takes the same amount of time regardless of the secret value.$q$),
('ci-q-1007','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',3,$q$An attacker collects thousands of power-consumption traces from a smart card performing the same cryptographic operation with different inputs, then applies statistical correlation techniques across the traces to extract the secret key, succeeding even though no single trace reveals anything by visual inspection. Which technique is this?$q$,$q$["Differential power analysis","Simple power analysis","Frequency analysis","A known-plaintext attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Differential power analysis (DPA) is defined by its use of statistical correlation across many collected traces to extract a key that isn't visible in any single trace. Simple power analysis (SPA), by contrast, relies on directly and visually reading distinguishing patterns from a single trace.$q$),
('ci-q-1008','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',2,$q$Why does a birthday attack let an attacker find a hash collision in roughly 2^(n/2) attempts instead of the 2^n attempts a brute-force search for one specific target hash would require?$q$,$q$["Because the attacker only needs any two inputs among many attempts to collide with each other, not a match to one predetermined target, similar to the odds that any two people in a room share a birthday","Because hash functions use half as many internal rounds as block ciphers","Because the attacker has access to a chosen-plaintext oracle for the hash function","Because collisions can only occur when the hash output is stored unsalted"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The birthday attack's speedup comes from comparing every generated input against every other generated input (any-pair matching), not against one fixed target, which mathematically reduces the expected number of attempts needed to find a collision to roughly the square root of the total possibilities, 2^(n/2) for an n-bit digest.$q$),
('ci-q-1009','cissp',3,'3.7','mcq',1,$q$A password-cracking tool uses a large precomputed set of hash-to-plaintext chains to look up a stolen password hash almost instantly, instead of hashing candidate passwords in real time. What is this technique called?$q$,$q$["A rainbow table attack","A birthday attack","A chosen-ciphertext attack","A man-in-the-middle attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Precomputing hash-to-plaintext mappings ahead of time so a captured hash can simply be looked up is the defining feature of a rainbow table attack, which trades storage and precomputation time for near-instant cracking at attack time.$q$),
('ci-q-1010','cissp',3,'3.7','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO attack types below in which the attacker actively chooses inputs to submit to an encryption or decryption oracle, rather than merely observing plaintext/ciphertext pairs that occur naturally.$q$,$q$["Chosen-plaintext attack","Chosen-ciphertext attack","Known-plaintext attack","Ciphertext-only attack"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Chosen-plaintext and chosen-ciphertext attacks both let the attacker actively submit chosen inputs to an oracle (an encryption oracle or a decryption oracle, respectively) and observe the outputs, giving far more power than a known-plaintext attack (limited to whatever pairs occur naturally) or a ciphertext-only attack (no plaintext access at all).$q$),
('ci-q-1011','cissp',3,'3.7','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO controls that directly help defend against side-channel attacks such as timing and power analysis.$q$,$q$["Constant-time cryptographic implementations that avoid secret-dependent branching or execution-time variation","Power-consumption masking or noise injection that obscures the correlation between operations and measurable power draw","Increasing the RSA modulus size from 2048 to 4096 bits","Salting password hashes before storage"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Constant-time code removes the timing signal an attacker could measure, and masking or noise injection obscures the power-consumption signal, directly addressing the physical leakage these attacks exploit. A larger RSA modulus increases resistance to brute-force factoring, and salting defeats rainbow tables - neither addresses physical side-channel leakage.$q$),
('ci-q-1012','cissp',3,'3.7','ordering',2,$q$Order these cryptanalytic attack models from the LEAST information or control available to the attacker to the MOST.$q$,$q$["Ciphertext-only attack","Known-plaintext attack","Chosen-plaintext attack","Chosen-ciphertext attack"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$This is the standard cryptanalysis strength hierarchy, from weakest to strongest: a ciphertext-only attacker has just captured ciphertext and nothing else; a known-plaintext attacker also has naturally occurring plaintext/ciphertext pairs; a chosen-plaintext attacker can actively submit plaintext to an encryption oracle; and a chosen-ciphertext attacker can submit ciphertext to a decryption oracle and observe the resulting plaintext, generally considered the most powerful practical attack model. A cipher secure against a stronger model is automatically secure against every weaker one.$q$),
('ci-q-1013','cissp',3,'3.7','matching',2,$q$Match each cryptanalytic attack to what the attacker possesses or controls.$q$,$q${"left":["Ciphertext-only attack","Known-plaintext attack","Chosen-plaintext attack","Man-in-the-middle attack"],"right":["The ability to submit arbitrary plaintext to an encryption oracle and observe the ciphertext","Interception and alteration of communications between two parties who believe they are talking directly","Only captured ciphertext, with no corresponding plaintext at all","Naturally occurring plaintext/ciphertext pairs the attacker did not choose"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A ciphertext-only attacker has nothing but intercepted ciphertext; a known-plaintext attacker additionally has plaintext/ciphertext pairs that occurred naturally, without any say in what they were; a chosen-plaintext attacker actively controls what plaintext is submitted to an oracle; and a man-in-the-middle attacker sits directly on the communication path, able to intercept and alter what each party sends. Each represents a distinct combination of access and control.$q$),
('ci-q-1014','cissp',3,'3.7','matching',3,$q$Match each side-channel or statistical attack to its primary mechanism.$q$,$q${"left":["Timing attack","Simple power analysis (SPA)","Differential power analysis (DPA)","Birthday attack"],"right":["Applies statistical correlation across many collected power traces to extract keys too subtle to see directly","Exploits the probability that two among many attempts collide, rather than matching one specific target","Infers key bits from measurable variation in how long an operation takes to execute","Directly reads a single power trace and visually correlates its pattern to the operation performed"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A timing attack infers key bits from execution-time variation; SPA directly reads and visually interprets a single power trace; DPA instead applies statistical correlation across many traces to pull out signals too subtle for direct inspection; and a birthday attack exploits any-pair collision probability rather than targeting one specific match. Each technique measures a different signal or exploits a different statistical property.$q$),
('ci-q-1015','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',1,$q$A campus redesigns its walkways, entrances, and low fencing so that visitors are naturally funneled past a staffed reception desk instead of being able to approach the building from many directions. Which CPTED strategy is being applied?$q$,$q$["Natural access control","Territorial reinforcement","Target hardening","Mechanical access control"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Guiding movement through design choices - path layout, landscaping, and entrance placement - without relying on a guard or locked door to physically stop people is natural access control. Territorial reinforcement is about ownership cues rather than movement, and target hardening or mechanical access control rely on barriers or devices rather than the site layout itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1016','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',2,$q$A facility posts clear property-line signage, keeps its grounds well maintained, and uses distinct paving to mark the transition from public sidewalk to company property. Which CPTED strategy does this best illustrate?$q$,$q$["Territorial reinforcement","Natural surveillance","Natural access control","Defensible space's safe adjoining areas element"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Signage, upkeep, and clear ownership markers that make a space visibly "belong" to someone and signal that a presence there will be noticed is the definition of territorial reinforcement, distinct from surveillance (observing an area) or access control (guiding movement through it).$q$),
('ci-q-1017','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',2,$q$According to broken windows theory, why does prompt repair of vandalism and litter removal support a facility's physical security posture?$q$,$q$["Visible neglect signals that no one is watching or cares, which invites further disorder and more serious crime, so maintenance itself acts as a deterrent","Vandalism repair is required by most fire codes regardless of security posture","Litter interferes with sprinkler system operation but has no link to future crime","Landscaping maintenance is unrelated to CPTED and only affects aesthetics"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Broken windows theory holds that visible signs of neglect signal an absence of oversight, encouraging escalating disorder; consistent maintenance is one of the concrete ways territorial reinforcement is expressed in practice, directly supporting deterrence rather than being a purely cosmetic concern.$q$),
('ci-q-1018','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',2,$q$A security team is deciding where to place perimeter fencing, entrance lighting, and camera coverage relative to a new office building. Applying Oscar Newman's defensible space theory, which factor should most directly influence how strongly employees feel a sense of ownership over, and willingness to monitor, a given area?$q$,$q$["The area's territoriality and image/milieu - whether the space visibly appears cared for and claimed by someone","The area's proximity to a public transit stop","The total square footage of the parking lot","The building's fire suppression system type"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Defensible space theory ties a sense of ownership directly to territoriality and to image/milieu (a well-maintained, evidently cared-for appearance) - factors that make occupants more likely to notice and act on something out of place. Transit proximity, lot size, and fire suppression type are unrelated to Newman's model.$q$),
('ci-q-1019','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',3,$q$A security consultant recommends heavy exterior bars on all ground-floor windows and an imposing 10-foot chain-link fence topped with razor wire around an otherwise open corporate campus. A CPTED-oriented reviewer objects. What is the most likely basis for that objection?$q$,$q$["The fortress-like appearance increases perceived fear and can undermine the natural surveillance and territorial cues CPTED strategies rely on, even though it may still be a necessary layer in genuinely high-risk areas","Bars and fencing are never an appropriate security control under any circumstances","CPTED strategies require that all physical barriers be removed entirely","Razor wire is illegal in all commercial applications"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CPTED does not prohibit target hardening outright, but treats aggressive hardening as a trade-off: a fortress-like campus signals fear, can visually block sightlines, and undermines the softer natural-surveillance and territorial cues the design otherwise relies on, so it is applied selectively rather than as the default approach.$q$),
('ci-q-1020','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',1,$q$An organization installs continuous, standby, and motion-triggered lighting across its parking areas as part of its site design. What is the primary security purpose of motion-triggered lighting specifically, compared to continuous lighting?$q$,$q$["It draws attention to activity in an otherwise unlit area while conserving energy the rest of the time","It replaces the need for any continuous lighting at the building entrance","It is required by CPTED in place of natural surveillance entirely","It automatically dims whenever occupancy sensors detect no vehicles present"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Motion-triggered lighting's specific value is drawing attention to unexpected activity precisely because the area is otherwise dark, while also saving energy compared to running full illumination continuously; it supplements, rather than replaces, continuous and standby lighting used elsewhere on the site.$q$),
('ci-q-1021','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',2,$q$A CPTED review of a corporate campus finds that mature trees planted decades ago now form a dense, low canopy that blocks sightlines from office windows down to the visitor parking lot below. What design change most directly restores natural surveillance in this scenario?$q$,$q$["Trimming the tree canopy so its lowest branches sit well above eye level, reopening sightlines to the parking lot","Replacing the surface parking lot with a multi-story parking structure","Increasing the height of the perimeter fence","Adding motion-activated door locks to the building entrance"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The problem described is specifically a blocked sightline caused by canopy height, so the direct fix is restoring that sightline by raising the canopy above eye level - the general CPTED guidance of trimming trees above roughly 6-8 feet. The other options address different concerns and don't restore the blocked view.$q$),
('ci-q-1022','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',3,$q$A city hardens physical security at a single retail location with new fencing, lighting, and staff presence. Over the following months, area crime statistics show that overall neighborhood crime did not decrease - offenders simply began targeting a similar, unprotected store two blocks away. What documented critique of site-hardening programs does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["Crime displacement","The birthday paradox","Territorial reinforcement failure","Frequency-analysis bias"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Crime displacement describes exactly this pattern: hardening a single, isolated site can simply push the same offenders and behavior toward a nearby, less-protected target rather than eliminating the underlying criminal activity, which is why CPTED is most effective applied consistently across an area rather than to one site in isolation.$q$),
('ci-q-1023','cissp',3,'3.8','mcq',1,$q$A data center campus organizes its physical space into a public parking and grounds area, a controlled lobby requiring badge check-in, a restricted office floor, and finally a highly secured server room requiring biometric authentication. What physical security design concept does this progression represent?$q$,$q$["Layered, concentric zones of physical security, applying defense-in-depth to physical space","A single flat perimeter with no internal boundaries","Target hardening applied only at the outermost layer","Crime displacement mitigation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Organizing a site into progressively more restricted, more strongly authenticated zones - from public grounds down to a critical asset like a server room - is the layered, concentric zone model, the physical-space equivalent of network defense-in-depth.$q$),
('ci-q-1024','cissp',3,'3.8','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO elements Oscar Newman identified as part of defensible space theory.$q$,$q$["Territoriality","Image/milieu (a well-maintained, cared-for appearance)","Encryption of building access logs","Mandatory armed guard presence at every entrance"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Newman's four defensible-space elements are territoriality, natural surveillance, image/milieu, and safe adjoining areas. Encryption of access logs is an information-security control unrelated to the theory, and defensible space theory does not require armed guards - it is about design-driven perception and ownership, not staffing levels.$q$),
('ci-q-1025','cissp',3,'3.8','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe how CPTED's natural strategies differ from target hardening.$q$,$q$["Natural strategies shape behavior and perception through design - sightlines, pathways, ownership cues; target hardening relies on physical barriers and force to deny access","Target hardening is sometimes still necessary as an additional layer, but is not itself one of CPTED's three core natural strategies","CPTED strategies always eliminate the need for locks, fences, or security guards entirely","Target hardening improves natural surveillance by adding more visible barriers to observe"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The correct pair captures the real relationship between the two approaches. The other two statements are false: CPTED does not eliminate the need for mechanical or organized controls entirely, and target hardening's barriers typically obstruct rather than improve natural surveillance.$q$),
('ci-q-1026','cissp',3,'3.8','ordering',2,$q$Order these physical security zones from least restrictive (outermost, public) to most restrictive (innermost, critical).$q$,$q$["Public/uncontrolled grounds and parking","Controlled zone - building perimeter and staffed lobby","Restricted zone - interior office floors for authorized staff only","Security/critical zone - data center or server room requiring the strongest authentication"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Layered physical security design places progressively stronger authentication and monitoring requirements moving inward: open public grounds, a controlled lobby where identity checks begin, restricted staff-only floors, and finally a critical zone protecting the most sensitive assets with the strongest authentication available.$q$),
('ci-q-1027','cissp',3,'3.8','matching',2,$q$Match each CPTED-related concept to an example that best illustrates it.$q$,$q${"left":["Natural surveillance","Natural access control","Territorial reinforcement","Target hardening (contrasted with CPTED's natural strategies)"],"right":["Designing a single, clearly visible walkway from the street to the main entrance instead of multiple unmonitored paths","Installing steel security bars over ground-floor windows","Positioning windows and low landscaping so employees can casually observe the parking lot from their desks","Posting property boundary signage and maintaining landscaping so the site visibly appears owned and cared for"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Natural surveillance is illustrated by sightlines preserved through window placement and low landscaping; natural access control by a single well-observed walkway; territorial reinforcement by signage and upkeep signaling ownership; and target hardening by a physical barrier like security bars, which sits outside CPTED's three natural strategies.$q$),
('ci-q-1028','cissp',3,'3.8','matching',3,$q$Match each physical security concept to its defining idea.$q$,$q${"left":["Defensible space theory","Broken windows theory","Crime displacement","Layered/concentric zones"],"right":["Progressively stronger authentication requirements moving from public to critical areas","Residents protect a space they feel ownership over and can observe","Hardening one site can simply shift offenders to a nearby, less-protected target","Visible disorder signals neglect and invites further, more serious crime"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Defensible space theory is Newman's claim that ownership and observability drive residents to protect a space; broken windows theory links visible neglect to escalating disorder; crime displacement describes hardening simply relocating offenders; and layered/concentric zones describe progressively stronger authentication moving inward through a facility.$q$),
('ci-q-1029','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',2,$q$A data center wants to minimize the risk of an accidental sprinkler discharge soaking equipment due to a single faulty sprinkler head, while still allowing water to enter the pipes quickly once a real fire is confirmed. Which sprinkler system design best fits this requirement?$q$,$q$["A pre-action system, requiring both a detection-system alarm and an individual sprinkler head trigger before water enters the pipes","A wet pipe system, since pipes are already filled with water at all times","A deluge system, since it covers the entire zone at once","A dry pipe system, used specifically because the facility is at freezing risk"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A pre-action system's defining feature is requiring two independent triggers - a detection-system alarm and the individual head's own heat trigger - before water even enters the pipes, directly minimizing the accidental-discharge risk a single faulty head poses in a wet pipe system, while still discharging promptly once a real fire is confirmed.$q$),
('ci-q-1030','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',2,$q$Why were Halon-based fire-suppression agents phased out under international agreement despite being highly effective and safe for electronics?$q$,$q$["They deplete stratospheric ozone","They are flammable and increase fire risk","They corrode server components on contact","They require an evacuation delay longer than any other agent"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Halon's phase-out under the Montreal Protocol was driven specifically by its ozone-depleting properties, not by any deficiency in fire-suppression performance or electronics safety - Halon was, and remains, chemically very effective at extinguishing fire without damaging equipment.$q$),
('ci-q-1031','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',3,$q$A facilities team is choosing between CO2 and a clean agent like FM-200 for an occupied network operations center staffed around the clock. Which factor should most strongly favor the clean agent over CO2?$q$,$q$["CO2 suppresses fire by displacing oxygen to a level that can asphyxiate people in the room, while FM-200 is designed to remain safe at its effective concentration for brief human exposure","FM-200 is cheaper to refill than CO2 in most facilities","CO2 cannot be used near any electronic equipment under any circumstances","FM-200 requires no fire-detection system to trigger discharge"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The decisive factor for an occupied space is human safety: CO2's oxygen-displacement mechanism is dangerous to occupants at extinguishing concentrations, while clean agents like FM-200 are specifically formulated and rated to remain safe for brief human exposure at the concentration needed to suppress fire, making them the appropriate choice for a continuously staffed room.$q$),
('ci-q-1032','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of maintaining positive air pressure inside a data center relative to surrounding spaces?$q$,$q$["To keep unfiltered outside air, dust, and contaminants from being drawn in through door openings and gaps","To increase the ambient temperature for equipment efficiency","To reduce the relative humidity level below 20%","To help the fire suppression system discharge more evenly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Positive pressurization pushes filtered, conditioned air outward through any gaps or openings, preventing unfiltered outside air, dust, and contaminants from being drawn in - it is an air-quality and particulate-control measure, not a temperature, humidity, or fire-suppression mechanism.$q$),
('ci-q-1033','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',1,$q$A data center's relative humidity has dropped well below the recommended 40-60% range during a dry winter. What risk does this primarily create for equipment?$q$,$q$["Increased buildup of static electricity, raising the risk of electrostatic-discharge damage to components","Increased corrosion of metal contacts and connectors","Condensation forming inside server chassis","Reduced effectiveness of the fire-suppression system"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Overly dry air lets static charge accumulate rather than bleed away harmlessly, raising the risk that a technician or a moving part discharges static electricity into sensitive components. Corrosion and condensation are risks of excessive humidity, the opposite failure mode.$q$),
('ci-q-1034','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',3,$q$A facility runs two complete, independently sized power paths - separate utility feeds, UPS units, generators, and distribution - each capable of carrying the entire data center's load on its own. What level of power redundancy does this describe, and what capability does it provide that N+1 redundancy does not?$q$,$q$["2N redundancy, allowing an entire power path to be fully taken offline for maintenance while the other path still carries 100% of the load","N+1 redundancy, allowing exactly one additional unit of spare capacity beyond normal load","2N+1 redundancy, a configuration only used at the very highest data center certification tiers","Single-path redundancy, which provides no protection against a full path failure"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$2N redundancy fully duplicates the entire power path end to end, so one complete path can fail or be taken offline for planned maintenance while the other independently carries the full load - a capability N+1 does not provide, since N+1 only tolerates the loss of a single spare unit within one shared path.$q$),
('ci-q-1035','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',1,$q$Which pairing correctly matches a facility power-protection component to the primary role it plays during a utility outage?$q$,$q$["A UPS bridges the immediate gap with battery power while a generator starts up and reaches full output","A generator bridges the immediate gap while a UPS sustains long-duration power for hours or days","A PDU generates backup power on-site during an outage","An EPO switch automatically restores power once utility service resumes"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The UPS's battery is already energized and can respond instantly at the moment of outage, covering the seconds-to-minutes gap before a generator starts and stabilizes; the generator then takes over for the outage's remaining, potentially much longer, duration. A PDU only distributes power, and an EPO switch cuts power manually rather than restoring it.$q$),
('ci-q-1036','cissp',3,'3.9','mcq',2,$q$A facility replaces its shared keypad cipher lock on the server room door with individual proximity badges issued to each employee. What security capability does this change primarily add?$q$,$q$["Individual accountability and an audit trail of exactly who entered and when","Elimination of the need for any physical door at all","Automatic fire-suppression activation whenever the door is opened","Reduced humidity inside the server room"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A shared cipher code cannot distinguish who used it, since everyone who knows the code enters identically; individual proximity badges tie each entry event to a specific credential, producing a per-person audit trail and enabling access to be revoked for one individual without affecting anyone else.$q$),
('ci-q-1037','cissp',3,'3.9','multi',1,$q$Select the TWO fire suppression systems that release water or agent only after a signal from a separate detection system, rather than relying solely on an individual sprinkler head's own heat-triggered element.$q$,$q$["Pre-action system","Deluge system","Wet pipe system","Dry pipe system"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A pre-action system requires a detection-system alarm before water can even enter the pipes, in addition to an individual head's own trigger. A deluge system uses open heads that release agent across an entire zone only once a separate detection system activates. Wet pipe and dry pipe systems both discharge based solely on an individual head's own heat-triggered element, with no separate detection-system signal required.$q$),
('ci-q-1038','cissp',3,'3.9','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO physical perimeter controls whose primary purpose is to physically stop or slow an intruder, rather than to observe, detect, or authenticate them.$q$,$q$["Bollards (vehicle-rated fixed posts)","Perimeter fencing rated for climb resistance","CCTV camera coverage","Proximity badge readers"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Bollards and climb-rated fencing are physical barriers whose function is to directly stop or slow entry. CCTV is a detective control - it observes and records but does not physically stop anyone - and badge readers are an authentication/authorization mechanism, not a physical barrier a determined intruder cannot bypass by force.$q$),
('ci-q-1039','cissp',3,'3.9','ordering',2,$q$Order these events by how quickly a facility's power-protection systems must typically respond to keep connected equipment running, from the fastest response required to the slowest.$q$,$q$["UPS battery power covering the instantaneous gap at the moment of outage","An automatic transfer switch detecting the outage and signaling the generator to start","The backup generator reaching full, stable output","Fuel resupply or utility power restoration for an extended outage"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$At the instant of outage, only the UPS's already-charged battery can respond with zero gap; within moments the automatic transfer switch detects the loss and signals the generator; the generator itself takes roughly seconds to a few minutes to reach stable full output; and only once an outage stretches on for hours does fuel resupply or utility restoration become the limiting concern. Each stage hands off to the next as its own time budget runs out.$q$),
('ci-q-1040','cissp',3,'3.9','matching',2,$q$Match each fire-suppression agent or approach to its defining characteristic.$q$,$q${"left":["Halon","FM-200 (a modern clean agent)","CO2","Water (wet pipe sprinklers)"],"right":["Effective and inexpensive, but conductive and damaging to energized electronic equipment","Suppresses fire by displacing oxygen, posing an asphyxiation risk to occupants","Highly effective chemical suppressant phased out due to ozone depletion","Clean agent designed to remain safe at effective concentration for brief human exposure"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Halon was an effective ozone-depleting agent phased out under the Montreal Protocol; FM-200 is a modern clean-agent replacement rated safe for brief human exposure; CO2 suppresses fire through oxygen displacement, which is exactly what makes it dangerous to occupants; and plain water, while cheap and effective on ordinary combustibles, is conductive and damages energized electronics, which is why data centers avoid direct water discharge onto live equipment.$q$),
('ci-q-1041','cissp',3,'3.9','matching',3,$q$Match each physical facility power component to its primary function.$q$,$q${"left":["Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS)","Backup generator","Power Distribution Unit (PDU)","Emergency Power Off (EPO) switch"],"right":["Distributes conditioned power from the main feed to individual racks or equipment","Allows immediate, manual, complete power cutoff to a room for life-safety reasons","Provides near-instantaneous battery power to bridge the gap at the moment of an outage","Sustains power for hours or days on fuel once started, after a short startup delay"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The UPS provides the instant, gap-free battery response at the moment of outage; the generator sustains power over the longer term once started; the PDU's role is distribution, not generation, moving conditioned power out to racks and equipment; and the EPO switch exists purely to allow an immediate, manual, total power cutoff for the safety of responders during an emergency.$q$),
('ci-q-1042','cissp',3,'3.9','matching',2,$q$Match each physical access control mechanism to its defining characteristic.$q$,$q${"left":["Mechanical key-and-tumbler lock","Cipher/keypad lock","Proximity badge/smart card reader","Biometric reader"],"right":["Ties access to a physical characteristic of the person, offering the strongest identity assurance","Basic physical control with no individual audit trail if keys are shared or copied","Grants access per individual credential and logs each entry attempt for accountability","Requires a shared numeric code, providing no per-person accountability"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A mechanical lock offers only basic control with no audit trail once keys are shared or copied; a cipher lock's shared code means anyone who knows it enters identically, with no way to tell users apart; a badge or smart-card reader ties each entry to an individual, logged credential; and a biometric reader ties access to the person's own physical characteristic, the strongest form of identity assurance among the four.$q$);
